{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c1","title":"Cartwright Act: ban on use or distribution of common pricing algorithms (AB 325)","requirement":"It is unlawful to use or distribute a common pricing algorithm as part of a contract, combination in the form of a trust, or conspiracy to restrain trade or commerce. It is also unlawful to use or distribute one while coercing another person to adopt the price or commercial term it recommends for the same or similar products or services in California.","key_value":"Common pricing algorithm = any methodology (including computer, software, or other technology) used by two or more persons that uses competitor data to recommend, align, stabilize, set, or otherwise influence a price or commercial term","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2026-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 16729 (added by Stats. 2025, ch. 338 (AB 325))","coverage_conditions":"Applies to any 'person' (as defined in Bus. & Prof. Code § 16702, excluding end consumers) who uses or distributes a common pricing algorithm for any product or service in California. This is general antitrust law, not specific to housing, but it reaches residential rental pricing. AB 325 also allows Cartwright Act complaints to plead only that a contract, combination, or conspiracy is plausible, without alleging facts that exclude independent action (Bus. & Prof. Code § 16756.1).","exemptions":"End consumers of a product or service are excluded from the definition of 'person'. An algorithm falls outside the definition unless two or more persons use it and it uses competitor data. Nothing in the section impairs or limits the applicability of antitrust laws.","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
